!function(){"use strict";var e={zh:{langSwitch:"EN",brandTitle:"中国民乐 · 数字乐器馆",headerHint:"点击乐器 · 进入乐器详情",enterDetail:"进入乐器详情",footer:"中国民乐 · 交互图鉴",footerNote:"点击中央乐器或下方乐器进入详情；在详情页滚轮缩放乐器图片、拖拽平移，下方专题切换内容",backShowcase:"返回展厅",zoneLegend:"滚轮 / 双指缩放 · 拖拽平移 · 全览复位",zoomReset:"全览",zone_history:"历史渊源",zone_construction:"构造与形制",zone_schools:"流派与风格",autoTour:"顺次参观三大专题",autoTouring:"顺次参观中…",fmLabel:"特色音乐",fmPlay:"▶ 点击播放",fmPlaying:"▶ 播放中",fmPaused:"⏸ 已暂停",theme:"主题曲",bgmOn:"背景音乐 · 梁祝",bgmOff:"背景音乐 · 已关闭",viewInstr:"查看",ariaStrip:"选择乐器"},en:{langSwitch:"中",brandTitle:"Chinese Folk Music · Digital Instrument Museum",headerHint:"Click an instrument to explore its details",enterDetail:"Enter Instrument",footer:"Chinese Folk Music · Interactive Archive",footerNote:"Click the central instrument or the strip below to open details; in detail view, scroll to zoom, drag to pan, and switch topics below",backShowcase:"Back to Hall",zoneLegend:"Scroll / pinch to zoom · drag to pan · reset to fit",zoomReset:"Fit",zone_history:"History",zone_construction:"Construction & Form",zone_schools:"Schools & Styles",autoTour:"Tour the three topics",autoTouring:"Touring…",fmLabel:"Featured Music",fmPlay:"▶ Play",fmPlaying:"▶ Playing",fmPaused:"⏸ Paused",theme:"Theme",bgmOn:"BGM · Liangzhu",bgmOff:"BGM · Off",viewInstr:"View",ariaStrip:"Select instrument"}},n={erhu:{name:"Erhu",category:"Bowed string",line:"Forefather of Huqin · Chinese Violin",altName:"Nanhu · Wengzi",summary:"Instrument 01 / 06 · Forefather of Huqin · Chinese Violin",intro:"Also known as Nanhu or Wengzi, the erhu is the most representative bowed string instrument of the huqin family. With a long history, its timbre is soft and profound, plaintive yet passionate, and it is often called the “Chinese violin.”",history:"The erhu’s predecessor can be traced to the xiqin of the Tang dynasty. It was called the jiqin in the Song dynasty and gradually evolved into the huqin after the Yuan dynasty. During the Ming and Qing dynasties, the erhu was widely used to accompany opera, storytelling, and folk music. In modern times, Liu Tianhua introduced the erhu into higher music education, composed the Ten Masterpieces of Erhu, and reformed the instrument; Hua Yanjun (A Bing) brought the erhu to the world with works such as Moon Reflected in Erquan.",construction:"The erhu consists mainly of the resonator, the python-skin head, the neck, the tuning pegs, the qianjin (string clamp), the strings, the bridge, the damping pad, and the bow. The resonator is the sound box, usually round, hexagonal, or octagonal, one end covered with python skin, which greatly affects the timbre. Two strings are stretched between two tuning pegs—an inner and an outer string. The qianjin fixes the effective string length, the bridge transmits vibration, and the damping pad controls noise and tone. The horsehair bow passes between the two strings to produce sound by friction.",schools:"In modern times the erhu developed into several schools, such as Wuxi, Jiang, Qin, Cantonese, Sichuan, and Henan. The Wuxi school, represented by Liu Tianhua and Hua Yanjun, is deep and delicate; the Jiang school, led by Jiang Fengzhi, is elegant and restrained; the Qin school, led by Lu Rirong, is high-pitched and spirited, blending elements of Qinqiang opera; the Cantonese school, led by Lü Wencheng, is bright and ornate with the charm of Guangdong music.",featuredMusic:"Horse Race (erhu)",zones:["Head · Tuning Pegs","Neck · Qianjin · Strings","Resonator · Skin · Bow"]},guzheng:{name:"Guzheng",category:"Plucked string",line:"King of Instruments · Oriental Piano",altName:"Qinzheng · Hanzheng",summary:"Instrument 02 / 06 · King of Instruments · Oriental Piano",intro:"Also known as the Qinzheng or Hanzheng, the guzheng is one of the most representative plucked string instruments of China. With a long history, its timbre is bright and flowing, blending strength with grace; it conveys the elegance of antiquity yet can play vigorous modern works, and is often called the “King of Instruments” or the “Oriental piano.”",history:"The guzheng has a history of more than 2,500 years. During the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods, it was already popular in the Qin region (present-day Shaanxi), hence the name Qinzheng. After the Qin and Han dynasties it spread across the Central Plains and flourished; during the Tang and Song dynasties it was used in court banquet music and folk music; after the Ming and Qing dynasties many regional schools arose. Historically the number of strings was not fixed—five, twelve, thirteen, sixteen, and so on—while the modern standard is twenty-one strings.",construction:"The guzheng is a rectangular wooden resonating box composed mainly of the soundboard, base board, side boards, front yueshan (bridge), rear yueshan, zheng bridges (yanzhu), strings, and tuning box. The soundboard is usually made of paulownia wood, the base of catalpa wood; the movable bridges, called yanzhu, are used to tune pitch. Modern strings are mostly steel-core nylon-wound, producing a bright and stable tone.",schools:"Regional schools of the guzheng include Shandong, Henan, Chaozhou, Hakka, Zhejiang, Shaanxi, and Fujian. The Shandong school is vigorous and brisk, the Henan school high-pitched and bold, the Chaozhou school delicate and mellifluous, the Hakka school plain and elegant, the Zhejiang school clear and fluent, and the Shaanxi school often carries a solemn and stirring spirit.",featuredMusic:"Sunset over Fishing Boats",zones:["Head · Yueshan","Soundboard · Bridges (Yanzhu)","Tail · Tuning Box"]},pipa:{name:"Pipa",category:"Plucked string",line:"King of Plucked Instruments",altName:"Pipa · Loquat",summary:"Instrument 03 / 06 · King of Plucked Instruments",intro:"Also known as Pipa or Loquat, it is one of the most representative plucked string instruments of China. With a long history, its timbre is crisp, bright, and richly textured; it can render the delicacy of literary pieces and the power of martial pieces alike, and is often called the “King of Plucked Instruments.”",history:"The word pipa first appears in Han records, originally a general term for various plucked instruments. In the Qin dynasty there was the xiantao; in the Han dynasty the Qin pipa and Han pipa. During the Northern and Southern dynasties, the curved-neck pipa was introduced from the Western Regions along the Silk Road—a pear-shaped body, four strings and four frets, held horizontally and played with a plectrum. The Tang dynasty was its golden age; it became a major instrument of court and folk music and gradually shifted from horizontal to vertical playing and from plectrum to fingers. From the Song to the Qing, the modern four-string, multi-fret pipa took shape. Modern musicians Liu Tianhua and Hua Yanjun also contributed importantly to pipa composition and performance.",construction:"The pipa is composed mainly of the head, neck, body, soundboard, back, xiang (large frets), pin (small frets), tuning pegs, strings, and string holder. The soundbox is pear-shaped, the soundboard usually paulownia, the back often of hardwoods such as rosewood. The neck and soundboard carry xiang and pin for fingering. The modern pipa has four strings and six xiang with twenty-four or twenty-five pin, giving a complete compass and easy transposition.",schools:"Modern pipa formed five major schools: Wuxi, Pinghu, Pudong, Chongming, and Wang. The Wuxi school, led by Hua Qiuping, is clear and elegant; the Pinghu school, led by Li Fangyuan, refined and delicate; the Pudong school, led by Ju Shilin and Shen Haochu, grand and vigorous; the Chongming school, led by Shen Zhaozhou, plain and classical; the Wang school, led by Wang Yuting, balances strength and grace. Each has its own repertoire, technique, and aesthetic.",featuredMusic:"Pipa Words (pipa)",zones:["Head · Neck · Xiang","Soundboard · Pin · Strings","String Holder · Body"]},dizi:{name:"Dizi",category:"Wind (bamboo flute)",line:"Soul of Folk Music",altName:"Bamboo Flute",summary:"Instrument 04 / 06 · Soul of Folk Music",intro:"Also known as the bamboo flute, it is one of the most representative wind instruments of China. With a long history, its timbre is crisp and sonorous, combining brightness with flexibility; it evokes the gentle elegance of the south and the bold passion of the northern frontier, and is often called the “Soul of Folk Music.”",history:"The history of the dizi goes back to the Neolithic era some eight to nine thousand years, when its earliest form, the bone flute, appeared. The Jiahu bone flutes unearthed at Wuyang, Henan, are among the world’s earliest playable instruments. Legend says bamboo flutes were made from the time of the Yellow Emperor; by the Qin and Han dynasties the bamboo flute had a mature form with seven holes; the Tang dynasty brought its golden age, popular at court and among the people; from the Song and Yuan it was widely used to accompany opera and developed regional styles.",construction:"The dizi is usually made from a single bamboo tube of purple bamboo or kuzhu. The tube carries a blowing hole, a membrane hole, finger holes, and an end hole. A thin membrane (dimo) is pasted over the membrane hole; its vibration is the key to the flute’s crisp, bright timbre. The tube is hollow, and its ends are often bound with silk thread and lacquered to prevent cracking and for decoration.",schools:"Modern dizi is divided mainly into a southern and a northern school. The southern school features the qudi, popular in Jiangsu, Zhejiang, and Guangdong, with a clear, elegant, and subtle style; representative players include Zhao Songting and Lu Chunling. The northern school features the bangdi, popular in Shandong, Hebei, and Shanxi, with a vigorous, unrestrained, and passionate style; representative players include Feng Zicun and Liu Guanyue.",featuredMusic:"Wu Ji (dizi)",zones:["Blowing End","Tube · Membrane Hole · Finger Holes","End Hole · Silk & Lacquer"]},xiao:{name:"Xiao",category:"Wind (vertical flute)",line:"Instrument of Scholars",altName:"Dongxiao",summary:"Instrument 05 / 06 · Instrument of Scholars",intro:"Also known as the dongxiao, it is one of the Chinese wind instruments richest in literary character. With a long history, its timbre is soft, deep, and tranquil, evoking distant and ethereal moods and expressing refined, subtle feeling; it is often called the “Instrument of Scholars.”",history:"The history of the xiao can be traced to the bone whistles and bone flutes of the Neolithic era, such as those unearthed at Hemudu, Zhejiang, and the Jiahu bone flutes of Wuyang, Henan—seven to nine thousand years old. In the time of Emperor Shun bamboo flutes were made for the Shao music, and in the Zhou dynasty the xiao entered the “eight timbres” classification. Archaeology reveals bone panpipes from the Warring States, and flute-playing scenes appear in Han dynasty murals. From the Wei, Jin, and Tang, the vertical flute was called di; in the Song and Yuan it was named xiao and yielded such types as yanyue xiao and guchui xiao. Since the Ming it was clearly distinguished from the transverse flute, and by the Qing its form matched the modern xiao. Historically it was also called chi or shu chi in the Han dynasty; the Western Jin musician Lie He and the Zhongshujian Xun Xu both improved it.",construction:"The xiao is usually made from a single bamboo tube of purple, white, or yellow dry bamboo, about 70–80 cm long. The upper end is sealed with a natural node, beside which a half-elliptical blowing hole is cut; six to eight finger holes are opened (six-hole: five front one back; eight-hole: seven front one back, the common modern form). The xiao has no membrane hole; the lower end is an open tube with a sound hole near the end. Professional instruments add a copper joint to adjust pitch; main varieties include the dongxiao, qinxiao, and yupingxiao.",schools:"Historically the xiao was more an object of emotional expression for scholars, with few dedicated solo pieces, so it never formed the sharply defined schools of the dizi. Yet in making and playing there remain southern and northern distinctions: the southern xiao (as in Fujian nanyin) has a thicker tube, greater tension, and a deep, full sound; the northern xiao (as in the qinxiao) has a thinner tube and an elegant, quiet timbre. The Gaoling dongxiao of Gaoling County, Xi’an, Shaanxi, is a representative of the northern school.",featuredMusic:"Qing Ping Yue (xiao)",historyImgCaption:"Neolithic bone whistle · Distant ancestor of the xiao",zones:["Blowing End · Sealed Node","Tube · Finger Holes · Copper Joint","Base · End Hole · Tassel"]},bianzhong:{name:"Bianzhong",category:"Percussion",line:"Ritual Bell Masterpiece",altName:"Bronze Chime Bells",summary:"Instrument 06 / 06 · Ritual Bell Masterpiece",intro:"Also known as bronze chime bells, the bianzhong is one of the most representative percussion instruments of ancient China. With a long history and majestic presence, its timbre is deep, resonant, solemn, and elegant; it can play melody and enrich harmony alike, and is often called the “Ritual Bell Masterpiece” and the “Eighth Wonder of the Ancient World.”",history:"The bianzhong first appeared in the Shang dynasty some 3,500 years ago, evolving from bronze bells (ling) and nao. In the Western Zhou, chime bells were incorporated into the ritual-music system as a symbol of aristocratic rank, usually in sets of three. In the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods they reached their peak, expanding to sets of nine or thirteen, with craftsmanship and tuning at their height. After the Qin and Han, as ritual music declined, bell making waned. From the Qing onward, bianzhong entered a period of renewed study and making. The set unearthed in 1978 from the tomb of Marquis Yi of Zeng at Suizhou, Hubei—65 bells—is the largest, best-preserved, and most complete in tuning ever found, representing the highest achievement of pre-Qin ritual music and bronze casting.",construction:"The bianzhong is cast in bronze. The bell body is hewa (gourd-shaped)—flat and round, like two tiles fitted together, unlike the round temple bell. Each part has a name: the wu is the flat top, the zheng the middle, the zhuan the narrow regions beside the zheng, the gu the lower striking area, and the xian the two ridges. The surface often carries fine panchi (coiled-serpent) and yunlei (cloud-thunder) patterns, along with inscriptions. The bells are hung in order of size and pitch on a large wooden-and-bronze frame with multiple tiers and faces. The Marquis Yi of Zeng set has three tiers in eight groups, sixty-five bells, and a frame more than seven meters long.",schools:"The bianzhong did not develop style-based schools like the dizi or guzheng, but regional sets differ in form and decoration. Those of the Central Plains follow the Zhou tradition—regular form, elegant ornament, the orthodoxy of Western Zhou ritual music. Sets of the southern Chu, Zeng, Cai, Wu, and Yue regions absorbed local elements: the Yue bells are flat and slender with short yong and fine yunlei or horizontal-S patterns; Chu sets also have distinctive arrangements, often in groups of eleven or thirteen. The Zeng set’s scale is built on gong, shang, zheng, and yu, with a low-register order centered on zheng that differs notably from Central Plains tradition, reflecting the fusion of northern and southern cultures.",featuredMusic:"Zen Bell · Zen Sound",historyImgCaption:"Chime bells from the tomb of Marquis Yi of Zeng · 65 bells · summit of pre-Qin ritual music",zones:["Upper Tier · Beam","Lower Tier · Gourd-Shaped Body","Frame Base · Mallet"]}},t="zh";try{var a=localStorage.getItem("minyue-locale");"en"!==a&&"zh"!==a||(t=a)}catch(e){}window.I18N={ui:e,instrEn:n,text:function(n){var a=e[t];return a&&null!=a[n]?a[n]:e.zh[n]||n},field:function(e,a){if("en"===t){var o=n[e.slug];if(o&&null!=o[a])return o[a]}return e[a]},zones:function(e){if("en"===t){var a=n[e.slug];if(a&&a.zones)return a.zones}return(e.zones||[]).map(function(e){return e.anchor})}},window.LOCALE=t,window.setLocale=function(e){if("zh"===e||"en"===e){t=e,window.LOCALE=e;try{localStorage.setItem("minyue-locale",e)}catch(e){}}}}();